  "worked_solution": "In the picture, we are given a sphere of radius $\\sqrt{2}$ centered at the origin.\n\nWe write the position of a point (vector) in spherical polar coordinates as $(\\rho ,\\theta ,\u03d5),$ where \n\n- $\\rho \\ge 0$ is the distance from the point to the origin,\n\n- $\\theta \\in [0,2\\pi )$ is the angle between the point and the positive $x$-axis, and\n\n- $\u03d5\\in [0,\\pi ]$ is the angle the corresponding vector makes with the positive $z$-axis.\n\nThe distance from any point to the origin on the given surface equals $\\sqrt{2}.$ On the other hand, $\\theta $ and $\u03d5$ vary arbitrarily in their respective domains. \n\nTherefore, the spherical polar equation of the surface is $\\rho =\\sqrt{2}.$",
